This version calculates an element's contribution when a smaller value reaches it. The stack keeps indices with increasing values. After popping an index, the new stack top is its left boundary and the current index is its right boundary.

A final cleanup pass uses `n` as the right boundary for values that were never popped during the main scan.

```java
class Solution {
    public int sumSubarrayMins(int[] arr) {
        final long MOD = 1000000007L;
        int n = arr.length;
        int[] stack = new int[n];
        int top = -1;
        long answer = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right <= n; right++) {
            while (top >= 0 && (right == n || arr[stack[top]] >= arr[right])) {
                int middle = stack[top--];
                int left = top >= 0 ? stack[top] : -1;

                long leftChoices = middle - left;
                long rightChoices = right - middle;
                long contribution = (leftChoices * rightChoices) % MOD;
                contribution = (contribution * arr[middle]) % MOD;
                answer = (answer + contribution) % MOD;
            }

            if (right < n) {
                stack[++top] = right;
            }
        }

        return (int) answer;
    }
}
```

The important edge case is a nondecreasing array. In that case, most values stay in the stack until the cleanup at `right == n`. A decreasing array causes many pops during the scan, but each index is still popped only once.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the index stack. The input array is not modified.

The value `endingSum` stores the sum of minimums for all subarrays that end at the current index. A stack helps find the previous index with a smaller value.

If that index is `previous`, then the subarrays beginning after it use the current value as their minimum. The other subarrays can reuse the sum already stored at `previous`.

For `[3, 1, 2, 4]`, the ending sums are `3, 2, 4, 8`, which add up to `17`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int sumSubarrayMins(int[] arr) {
        final long MOD = 1000000007L;
        int n = arr.length;
        long[] endingSum = new long[n];
        int[] stack = new int[n];
        int top = -1;
        long answer = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            while (top >= 0 && arr[stack[top]] >= arr[i]) {
                top--;
            }

            int previous = top >= 0 ? stack[top] : -1;
            long current = (long) (i - previous) * arr[i];

            if (previous >= 0) {
                current += endingSum[previous];
            }

            endingSum[i] = current % MOD;
            answer = (answer + endingSum[i]) % MOD;
            stack[++top] = i;
        }

        return (int) answer;
    }
}
```

The `>=` comparison removes equal values before using the stack top. This gives one consistent owner for subarrays containing duplicate minimums.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since each index enters and leaves the stack at most once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack and the ending sums. The input array is not changed.

Here the stack stores pairs of `(value, count)` instead of only indices. `endingSum` is the sum of minimums for all subarrays ending at the current position.

When a larger or equal value is popped, its group is replaced by the current smaller value. The current value then becomes the minimum for all those groups plus the one-element subarray.

For `arr = [3, 1, 2]`, the running sums are `3`, `2`, and `4`. Their total is `9`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int sumSubarrayMins(vector<int> &arr) {
        const long long MOD = 1000000007LL;
        vector<pair<int, int>> stack;
        long long endingSum = 0;
        long long answer = 0;

        for (int value : arr) {
            int count = 1;

            while (!stack.empty() && stack.back().first >= value) {
                endingSum -= 1LL * stack.back().first * stack.back().second;
                count += stack.back().second;
                stack.pop_back();
            }

            stack.push_back({value, count});
            endingSum = (endingSum + 1LL * value * count) % MOD;
            answer = (answer + endingSum) % MOD;
        }

        return static_cast<int>(answer);
    }
};
```

The stack groups consecutive choices that currently have the same minimum. This keeps the code to one pass and avoids separate left and right arrays. The input array is read only.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, because each group is pushed and popped at most once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` in the worst case when the stack keeps many groups.

Instead of checking each subarray, count how many ranges use each element as their minimum. For an index `i`, the left boundary is the previous smaller or equal value, and the right boundary is the next strictly smaller value.

The number of choices is `(i - left[i]) * (right[i] - i)`. Equal values use different comparisons on the two passes so a subarray is not counted twice.

Dry run for `arr = [3, 1, 2, 4]`:

- For `2`, `left = 1` and `right = 4`.
- It has `2 - 1 = 1` left choice and `4 - 2 = 2` right choices.
- Its contribution is `2 * 1 * 2 = 4`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int sumSubarrayMins(vector<int> &arr) {
        const long long MOD = 1000000007LL;
        int n = static_cast<int>(arr.size());
        vector<int> left(n), right(n);
        stack<int> indices;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            while (!indices.empty() && arr[indices.top()] > arr[i]) {
                indices.pop();
            }

            left[i] = indices.empty() ? -1 : indices.top();
            indices.push(i);
        }

        while (!indices.empty()) {
            indices.pop();
        }

        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            while (!indices.empty() && arr[indices.top()] >= arr[i]) {
                indices.pop();
            }

            right[i] = indices.empty() ? n : indices.top();
            indices.push(i);
        }

        long long answer = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            long long leftChoices = i - left[i];
            long long rightChoices = right[i] - i;
            long long contribution = (leftChoices * rightChoices) % MOD;
            contribution = (contribution * arr[i]) % MOD;
            answer = (answer + contribution) % MOD;
        }

        return static_cast<int>(answer);
    }
};
```

For an empty array, both passes simply do no work and the answer remains zero. The platform normally provides a non-empty array, but this code also handles that case.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the two boundary arrays and the stack. The input array is not changed.

